<header>
  <h1>U.S. Inflation Dashboard</h1>
  <p>Figures below trace directly to federal statistical agencies — click through to verify each number at its origin.</p>
  <div class="asof">Latest official data pulled: September 1, 2026 · adds July JOLTS (released today) and continued Iran/Hormuz escalation, incl. new threats to Kharg Island · Next: Aug payrolls (Sept 4), CPI Aug data (Sept 11)</div>
</header>

<main id="main-content">

<section>
  <h2>Summary — where things stand right now</h2>
  <p class="subnote">The dashboard's ~20 metrics, boiled down. Every number links to its full section below for sourcing and detail.</p>

  <div class="summary-box">
    <div class="sb-top"><span class="sb-metric"><a href="#sec-cpi-trend" class="sb-link">Headline inflation (CPI) →</a></span><span class="sb-value down">3.4%</span></div>
    <div class="sb-impact">Cooling for 3 straight months, but real wages are still falling — prices are rising slower, not actually dropping. Your dollar buys a bit less than it did a year ago, just less badly than in spring.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="summary-box">
    <div class="sb-top"><span class="sb-metric"><a href="#sec-groceries" class="sb-link">Groceries →</a></span><span class="sb-value flat">3.0%</span></div>
    <div class="sb-impact">Food costs are outpacing wage growth for many households. Combined with 4.5M+ people losing SNAP benefits, grocery budgets are under real, compounding pressure right now.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="summary-box">
    <div class="sb-top"><span class="sb-metric"><a href="#sec-housing" class="sb-link">Housing (rent/shelter) →</a></span><span class="sb-value up">3.2%</span></div>
    <div class="sb-impact">Still the single biggest driver of your cost of living, even as it slowly cools. If you're renting or plan to buy soon, this is the number that affects you most directly, more than any other on this page.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="summary-box">
    <div class="sb-top"><span class="sb-metric"><a href="#sec-mortgage" class="sb-link">Mortgage rate (30-yr) →</a></span><span class="sb-value up">6.67%</span></div>
    <div class="sb-impact">Homebuying got modestly more expensive again this month. On a $400,000 loan, the difference between 6.5% and 6.7% is roughly $50/month.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="summary-box">
    <div class="sb-top"><span class="sb-metric"><a href="#sec-energy" class="sb-link">Gasoline →</a></span><span class="sb-value down">24.6%</span></div>
    <div class="sb-impact">Still up sharply year-over-year from the Iran conflict, but easing fast. Expect this number to keep shrinking through fall — it's the main reason headline inflation is falling right now.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="summary-box">
    <div class="sb-top"><span class="sb-metric"><a href="#sec-unemployment" class="sb-link">Unemployment →</a></span><span class="sb-value down">4.1%</span></div>
    <div class="sb-impact">Looks good, isn't really. The economy lost jobs in July; the rate only fell because people stopped looking for work. If you're job-hunting, this is a harder market than the headline number suggests.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="summary-box">
    <div class="sb-top"><span class="sb-metric"><a href="#sec-unemployment" class="sb-link">Real wage growth →</a></span><span class="sb-value up">-0.2 pts</span></div>
    <div class="sb-impact">Paychecks are growing slower than prices for a 4th straight month. This is arguably the single most important number for household budgets on this whole page.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="summary-box">
    <div class="sb-top"><span class="sb-metric"><a href="#sec-home-prices" class="sb-link">Home prices (sale value) →</a></span><span class="sb-value flat">1.5%</span></div>
    <div class="sb-impact">If you own a home, its real (inflation-adjusted) value has fallen for 13 straight months, even though the price tag looks higher. Don't confuse rising rent with rising home equity — they're moving differently.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="summary-box">
    <div class="sb-top"><span class="sb-metric"><a href="#sec-medical" class="sb-link">Medical care →</a></span><span class="sb-value down">1.7%</span></div>
    <div class="sb-impact">The one clearly good number here — healthcare cost growth has slowed to its lowest pace of 2026. Doesn't offset the loss of SNAP or the uninsured-rate trend below, but it's genuinely encouraging.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="summary-box">
    <div class="sb-top"><span class="sb-metric"><a href="#sec-uninsured" class="sb-link">Uninsured rate →</a></span><span class="sb-value flat">8.3%</span></div>
    <div class="sb-impact">Flat for now, but expected to climb — expired ACA subsidies and Medicaid changes are still phasing in. If you or family members rely on marketplace coverage, budget for higher premiums ahead.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="note warn" style="margin-top:14px;">
    <strong>The one-sentence version:</strong> inflation is cooling but hasn't stopped, wages aren't keeping up, the job market is weaker than the unemployment rate suggests, and the safety net (SNAP, ACA subsidies) is shrinking at the same time — meaning the people most exposed to slower price growth are also losing some of the support that used to cushion it.
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>A note on data reliability</h2>
  <div class="note warn">
    Two things worth knowing before trusting any single month's number too much. First, the 2025 government shutdown caused BLS to skip publishing October and November 2025 CPI data entirely — those gaps distort some of the year-over-year comparisons still working through the system in 2026. Second, a federal watchdog report found that while BLS "took steps to fix data release failures," it concluded more safeguards are still needed — a reminder that even official releases carry some operational risk right now. None of this means the numbers below are wrong, just that they deserve the usual caution around revisions (CPI data can be revised for up to 5 years; jobs data gets revised monthly, sometimes by over 100,000 as happened with May/June 2026).
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 id="sec-cpi-trend">3-month trend — headline &amp; core inflation</h2>
  <p class="subnote">All figures are 12-month (year-over-year) percent change, not seasonally adjusted, from BLS CPI news releases.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap"><div class="scroll-hint">↔ swipe to see all columns</div><div class="table-scroll"><table class="trend-table">
    <tr>
      <th>Measure</th><th>May 2026</th><th>June 2026</th><th>July 2026</th><th>Trend</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td class="metric">CPI-U, all items (headline)</td>
      <td>4.2%</td><td>3.5%</td><td>3.4%</td>
      <td class="arrow-down">↓ third straight decline</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td class="metric">Core CPI (excl. food &amp; energy)</td>
      <td>2.9%</td><td>2.6%</td><td>2.5%</td>
      <td class="arrow-down">↓ easing steadily</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td class="metric">Food (all items)</td>
      <td>3.1%</td><td>3.0%</td><td>3.0%</td>
      <td class="arrow-flat">↔ steady</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td class="metric">Medical care (CPI)</td>
      <td>2.6%</td><td>2.0%</td><td>1.7%</td>
      <td class="arrow-down">↓ cooling further</td>
    </tr>
  </table></div></div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:12px;">
    Headline CPI has now cooled for three straight months, but July's report showed the deceleration losing steam: prices rose 0.1% for the month, and the annual rate barely budged, from 3.5% to 3.4%. Gasoline kept falling (-2.9% for the month, still up 24.6% year-over-year), but a new concern surfaced — inflation is now outpacing wage growth. Average hourly earnings were up 3.2% year-over-year, below the 3.4% CPI rate, meaning real wages actually fell for a fourth straight month. Fed officials are split on what to do about it: three regional presidents dissented at the July 29 meeting, pushing for a rate hike, while Chair Kevin Warsh held rates steady at 3.50%-3.75% for a fifth consecutive meeting.
  </div>
  <div class="impact"><span class="impact-label">What this means for you</span>Your overall cost of living is still rising, just more slowly than in spring. If your raises or fixed income haven't kept pace with about 3.4% growth over the past year, you're effectively behind — and July's data shows wages already are.</div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 id="sec-pce">PCE — the Fed's actual preferred gauge</h2>
  <p class="subnote">Personal Consumption Expenditures price index, from BEA's Personal Income and Outlays report. This is the inflation measure the Fed's 2% target is actually defined against — not CPI.</p>
  <table class="trend-table">
    <tr>
      <th>Measure</th><th>Apr 2026</th><th>May 2026</th><th>Jun 2026</th><th>Jul 2026</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td class="metric">Core PCE, 12-mo</td>
      <td>3.3%</td><td>3.4%</td><td>3.3%</td><td>3.3%</td>
    </tr>
  </table>
  <div class="stat-grid" style="margin-top:14px;">
    <div class="stat">
      <div class="label">Headline PCE, 12-mo</div>
      <div class="value" style="color:var(--up);">3.7%</div>
      <div class="period">July 2026, above forecast</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
      <div class="label">Personal income, monthly</div>
      <div class="value" style="color:var(--up);">+0.4%</div>
      <div class="period">July 2026</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
      <div class="label">Real spending, monthly</div>
      <div class="value" style="color:var(--flat);">~0.0%</div>
      <div class="period">barely moved despite income growth</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="note warn" style="margin-top:12px;">
    This is arguably the most important number added to this dashboard so far: core PCE has now been stuck at 3.3-3.4% for four straight months (April through July) with essentially no net improvement. That stall — not any single month's CPI reading — is what pushed Fed Chair Kevin Warsh toward a notably hawkier tone at Jackson Hole this week (see the Federal Reserve section below). Separately, July's report showed incomes rising twice as fast as actual spending, an unusual combination that suggests households are saving more cautiously rather than spending freely, even as their paychecks grow.
  </div>
  <div class="impact"><span class="impact-label">What this means for you</span>The Fed pays more attention to this number than to the CPI headlines. A stalled core PCE reading makes a September rate cut much less likely — and modestly raises the odds of a rate hike instead, which would mean borrowing costs (credit cards, auto loans, variable mortgages) stay elevated or climb further.</div>
  <div class="source-card" style="margin-top:12px;">
    <a href="https://www.bea.gov/data/personal-consumption-expenditures-price-index" target="_blank">BEA — Personal Consumption Expenditures Price Index</a>
    <div class="desc">Released monthly as part of the Personal Income and Outlays report; the Fed's statutory 2% inflation target is defined using this index.</div>
    <span class="tag">bea.gov</span>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Chained CPI &amp; what it means for your paycheck</h2>
  <div class="stat-grid">
    <div class="stat">
