      <div class="period">began Feb 28, 2026 — escalating again</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="note warn" style="margin-top:12px;">
    <strong>Update, Sept 1:</strong> the escalation that began Aug 31 has continued to intensify. President Trump reportedly vowed to "hit them very hard" after Iran struck two U.S. bases in Jordan, and threats have now extended to Kharg Island — the terminal handling roughly 90% of Iran's oil exports (about 7 million barrels/day of capacity). Separately, refinery strikes inside Russia have tightened global refining capacity on top of the Hormuz disruption, and reporting indicates the U.S. Strategic Petroleum Reserve has been drawn down close to minimum operational levels amid increased crude exports. A supertanker fire from a mine strike in the southern Strait, reported yesterday, has not been independently confirmed. This is now a broader supply-side story than the Strait alone — worth watching whether Kharg Island threats materialize, since that would be a significant step beyond the shipping-lane disruption this dashboard has tracked so far.
  </div>
  <div class="note warn" style="margin-top:12px;">
    <strong>Update, August 31:</strong> fighting resumed today after several weeks of relative calm. U.S. forces struck Iranian rocket launchers near the Strait that were reportedly preparing to deploy mines, and Iran claimed retaliatory attacks on U.S. bases in Jordan; Iran also reported a supertanker struck two mines on Hormuz's southern route, though that's unconfirmed independently. Oil jumped in response (see Energy section above). This ends a period where oil had actually been trending down through most of August as diplomacy seemed possible — a reminder that this situation can reverse in a single day, in either direction.
  </div>
  <div class="note warn" style="margin-top:12px;">
    <strong>Correction from an earlier version of this dashboard:</strong> a prior update described a "ceasefire" that reopened the Strait of Hormuz in late June. That was inaccurate. The Strait has instead been in a state of on-and-off crisis since Iranian forces effectively closed it on March 4, 2026 — insurance for ships transiting the Strait is unavailable or prohibitively expensive, and traffic remains severely disrupted, though analysts estimate 6-8 million barrels/day (down from a pre-war 20-24 million) still move through via other Gulf producers. Economists at CEPR estimate the disruption alone is adding roughly 0.6 percentage points to 2026 headline CPI and 0.2 points to core CPI — a meaningful, quantifiable share of the inflation tracked throughout this dashboard. This is a live, unresolved situation, not a resolved one, and energy figures above should be read with that in mind.
  </div>
