  <div class="note warn" style="margin-top:12px;">
    July's jobs report was the clearest warning sign yet. The economy unexpectedly <strong>lost</strong> 23,000 jobs — economists had forecast a gain of roughly 83,000 — and BLS revised May and June down by a combined 103,000. The unemployment rate fell to 4.1%, but for the same reason it's been falling all summer: people leaving the workforce, not stronger hiring. Labor force participation is now at 61.4%, its lowest point since early 2021 and, outside the pandemic, the lowest since the mid-1970s. Average hourly earnings growth (3.2%) is now running below CPI inflation (3.4%), meaning real wages have declined for four consecutive months.
  </div>
  <div class="note warn" style="margin-top:12px;">
    <strong>Watch this date:</strong> BLS will publish a preliminary annual benchmark revision to the establishment (payroll) survey on August 28, 2026. These revisions true up the monthly estimates against more complete state unemployment-insurance tax records, and given how much May and June were already revised down this summer, there's a real chance July's already-weak reading gets revised weaker still. The final benchmark revision won't land until the January 2027 jobs report.
