  <div class="note warn" style="margin-top:12px;">
    <strong>The Fed's tone shifted hawkish this week.</strong> At the Jackson Hole Economic Policy Symposium on August 28, Chair Kevin Warsh delivered his most detailed remarks yet, saying this summer's inflation readings "do not tell me that underlying trends have meaningfully improved" and that inflation is "more concerning" than labor-market softness. He stopped short of committing to a hike, sticking to his pattern of withholding forward guidance — but bond markets reacted immediately: two-year Treasury yields jumped, and CME futures now price the odds of a September rate <em>hike</em> at roughly a coin flip, up sharply from about one-third before he spoke. Some economists (e.g., Navy Federal's Heather Long) think a hike is more likely in October or December than September. This is a real reversal from a month ago, when markets were debating whether the Fed's next move would be a cut.
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:12px;">
    Context for the split: three regional bank presidents — Cleveland's Beth Hammack, Minneapolis's Neel Kashkari, and Dallas's Lorie Logan — already dissented at the July 29 meeting, wanting to raise rates. Warsh's Jackson Hole remarks suggest he may now be leaning toward their view, especially with core PCE stalled at 3.3-3.4% for four straight months (see the PCE section above).
  </div>
